{{also|tally-ho|tally ho}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|tally-ho}}

===Etymology===
1772, UK, probably alteration of {{uder|en|fr|taïaut|pos=interjection used in deer-hunting}},<ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster Online}}</ref> from {{uder|en|frm|tahou}}, {{m|frm|tayo}}, from {{uder|en|fro|taho}}, {{m|fro|ta ho}}, {{m|fro|tielau|pos=interjection given to hounds to return}} (second half of 13th century), composed of {{m|fro|ta|pos=particle used to prod animals}} + {{m|fro|ho|ho !|halt!, hold!}}.<ref>{{R:fr:TLFi|taïaut|etym=1}}</ref> More at {{m|en|ho}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-us-tallyho.oga|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-tallyho.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|UK}} Used to [[urge]] on a [[fox hunt]], especially when the [[fox]] is [[sight]]ed.
#: ''There he is! Coming out of that coppice! '''Tallyho tallyho tallyho!'''
# A cry used to signify encouragement when embarking on any somewhat daring endeavour.
#* {{quote-av|1=en|year=1966|director={{w|Bruce Brown (director)|Bruce Brown}}|title={{w|The Endless Summer}}|text=Here goes the Lord with one of his famous "Tallyho" pullouts. '''Tallyho!'''}}
# A simple greeting, primarily used by the [[upper class]]es.
#: {{ux|en|'''Tallyho''', chaps! What's the time, anybody?}}
# {{lb|en|radio|aviation}} [[target|Target]] sighted.
#: ''(Air Traffic Control): Speedbird 123, New York, traffic at two o’clock, seven miles, a Boeing 737, west-bound, at 4000 feet.”''
#: ''(Pilot): New York, Speedbird 123, '''tallyho'''.''

====Usage notes====
In aviation radio usage, typically abbreviated to {{m|en|tally}}. In civilian aviation usage, the official term for “traffic sighted” is “traffic in sight”.<ref>Federal Aviation Administration: [https://web.archive.org/web/20141013230622/http://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/PCG/Index.htm Pilot/Controller Glossary (P/CG)], [https://web.archive.org/web/20140912024001/http://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/ATPubs/PCG/T.HTM T] (Traffic)</ref>

